window.__hvBdRegister("euclid-elements", {"key":"euclid-elements","sections":[{"i":0,"j":122,"loc":"Book I, Definitions 1–23, Postulates, Common Notions, Propositions 1–5","title":"First principles and the opening constructions","summary":"After a short introduction to Heath's translation, Book I sets out 23 definitions (point, line, surface, angle, circle, the kinds of triangle and quadrilateral, parallels), five postulates and five common notions. Proposition 1 constructs an equilateral triangle on a given straight line using two circles; Propositions 2 and 3 transfer a given length to a given point and cut off a shorter line from a longer one. Proposition 4 proves, by applying one triangle to another, that two sides and the included angle fix the triangle (side-angle-side). Proposition 5 proves that the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal, as are the angles under the base.","ideas":["A point has no part; a line is breadthless length","The fifth postulate: lines meet on the side where interior angles fall short of two right angles","Every step cites a definition, postulate, common notion or earlier proposition","Superposition of triangles proves side-angle-side congruence (I.4)"],"terms":[{"t":"aitēma (postulate)","d":"A construction or claim asked to be granted, such as drawing a straight line between any two points."},{"t":"koinē ennoia (common notion)","d":"A general axiom about magnitudes, such as that things equal to the same thing are equal to one another."},{"t":"parallel straight lines","d":"Straight lines in one plane that never meet however far they are produced in either direction."}]},{"i":123,"j":232,"loc":"Book I, Propositions 6–13","title":"Converse of isosceles theorem and basic constructions","summary":"Proposition 6 proves the converse of I.5: equal angles in a triangle are subtended by equal sides, shown by reduction to absurdity. Propositions 7 and 8 establish side-side-side congruence, I.7 showing that two triangles with equal sides cannot stand on the same base and same side with different vertices. Euclid then bisects a given angle (9) and a given straight line (10), erects a perpendicular from a point on a line (11) and drops one from a point outside it (12). Proposition 13 proves that a straight line standing on another makes either two right angles or angles together equal to two right angles.","ideas":["Proof by reduction to the impossible (the less equal to the greater)","Side-side-side congruence (I.8) rests on I.7","Bisections and perpendiculars are all built from the equilateral triangle of I.1","Adjacent angles on a straight line sum to two right angles"],"terms":[{"t":"Q. E. D. / Q. E. F.","d":"Heath's abbreviations for 'what it was required to prove' (theorems) and 'what it was required to do' (problems)."},{"t":"perpendicular (kathetos)","d":"A straight line standing on another so that the adjacent angles are equal, each being right."}]},{"i":233,"j":403,"loc":"Book I, Propositions 14–25","title":"Vertical angles and inequalities in triangles","summary":"Proposition 14 gives the converse of I.13, and I.15 proves vertical angles equal, with a porism that angles round a point make four right angles. Proposition 16 proves the exterior angle of a triangle greater than either interior opposite angle, from which I.17 shows any two angles of a triangle are less than two right angles. Propositions 18–21 relate greater sides to greater angles and prove that two sides of a triangle together exceed the third. Proposition 22 constructs a triangle from three given lines, stating the condition for it to be possible; I.23 copies a given angle, and I.24–25 compare bases and included angles of triangles with two equal sides.","ideas":["Exterior angle exceeds each interior opposite angle (I.16)","The greater side subtends the greater angle, and conversely","Triangle inequality (I.20)","A problem's solution may require a stated condition of possibility (I.22)"],"terms":[{"t":"porism","d":"A corollary that follows at once from a proposition just proved."},{"t":"diorismos","d":"A statement of the conditions under which a construction is possible, first seen in I.22."}]},{"i":404,"j":529,"loc":"Book I, Propositions 26–33","title":"Angle-side congruence and the theory of parallels","summary":"Proposition 26 proves congruence when two angles and one side, adjoining or opposite, are equal. Propositions 27 and 28 show that equal alternate angles, or an exterior angle equal to the interior opposite one, make two lines parallel; the proof uses I.16, not the fifth postulate. Proposition 29, the converse, is the first to use Postulate 5. From it follow I.30 (lines parallel to the same line are parallel), I.31 (drawing a parallel through a point), I.32 (the angles of a triangle equal two right angles, the exterior angle equals the two interior opposite) and I.33 (lines joining equal parallel lines are equal and parallel).","ideas":["Angle-angle-side congruence (I.26)","Parallelism is proved from angles before Postulate 5 is invoked","I.29 is the first use of the parallel postulate","Angle sum of a triangle is two right angles (I.32)"],"terms":[{"t":"alternate angles (enallax)","d":"Angles on opposite sides of a transversal and within the two lines it crosses."},{"t":"transversal","d":"A straight line falling across two other straight lines."}]},{"i":530,"j":663,"loc":"Book I, Propositions 34–43","title":"Parallelograms and triangles equal in area","summary":"Proposition 34 proves that the opposite sides and angles of a parallelogram are equal and that the diameter bisects it. Propositions 35–38 show that parallelograms, and triangles, on the same or equal bases and in the same parallels are equal in area. Propositions 39–40 give the converses: equal triangles on the same or equal bases and on the same side lie in the same parallels. Proposition 41 proves a parallelogram double a triangle on the same base in the same parallels; I.42 constructs a parallelogram equal to a given triangle in a given angle, and I.43 proves the complements about a diameter equal.","ideas":["'Equal' here means equal in area, not congruent","Shapes between the same parallels on equal bases are equal","A parallelogram is double the triangle on its base","Complements about the diameter are equal (I.43)"],"terms":[{"t":"parallelogrammic area","d":"Euclid's first name for a four-sided figure bounded by two pairs of parallel lines."},{"t":"paraplērōma (complement)","d":"Either of the two parallelograms filling the gaps beside the parallelograms about a diameter."}]},{"i":664,"j":764,"loc":"Book I, Propositions 44–48","title":"Application of areas and the Pythagorean theorem","summary":"Proposition 44 applies to a given straight line, in a given angle, a parallelogram equal to a given triangle, using the complements of I.43 and Postulate 5. Proposition 45 extends this to any rectilineal figure by cutting it into triangles. Proposition 46 constructs a square on a given straight line. Proposition 47 proves that in a right-angled triangle the square on the side opposite the right angle equals the squares on the other two sides, by dividing the large square into two parallelograms equal to the smaller squares. Proposition 48 proves the converse, closing Book I.","ideas":["Application of areas: fitting a given area to a given line","Any rectilineal figure can be turned into an equal parallelogram","I.47: the square on the hypotenuse equals the squares on the sides","I.48: the converse also holds"],"terms":[{"t":"parabolē (application)","d":"Constructing on a given straight line a parallelogram equal to a given area in a given angle."},{"t":"hypotenuse","d":"The side subtending the right angle, as Euclid describes it in I.47."}]},{"i":765,"j":803,"loc":"Book I, Endnotes (Propositions 1–15)","title":"Heath's notes on Greek idiom, Book I","summary":"Heath's endnotes explain how he translated the Greek of I.1–15. He discusses the perfect passive imperative used in constructions, his insertion of 'straight lines' and 'sides', and why he abandons 'each to each' for 'respectively'. He reports Proclus on the meaning of 'base', and Heiberg's evidence that a clause in I.4 about two lines enclosing a space is an early interpolation. On I.7 he defends adding words to an elliptical enunciation, cites Aristotle's Meteorologica, and recounts Thomas Taylor's quarrel with Simson over Proclus. Further notes treat phrasing such as 'much greater', the use of Postulate 4 in I.14, and Proclus's distinction between adjacent and vertical angles.","ideas":["Literal translation of Euclid's elliptical Greek is often impossible","Some phrases are later scholia or interpolations (Heiberg)","Technical phrasing in I.7 was already traditional by Aristotle's time","Taylor defended Proclus against Simson's dismissive remarks"],"terms":[{"t":"gegraphthō","d":"'Let it have been described': the perfect passive imperative Euclid uses in constructions."},{"t":"interpolation","d":"Words added to the text by a later hand, which Heath and Heiberg bracket or discuss."}]},{"i":804,"j":841,"loc":"Book I, Endnotes (Propositions 16–48)","title":"Heath's notes on Book I, Propositions 16–48","summary":"The notes continue with Greek terms for exterior and interior angles and the subtle wording that tells I.18 from I.19. Heath reports Proclus's answer to the Epicureans, who mocked I.20 as obvious even to an ass, and notes I.22 as the first diorismos in the Elements. He explains why Euclid gives only one case in I.24, explains Proclus's account of 'alternate' angles, and notes that the word 'parallelogram' was first introduced by Euclid according to Proclus. Other notes bracket interpolated appeals to interpolated common notions, explain 'complements', and contrast 'constructing' a triangle with 'describing' a square in I.46–47.","ideas":["Perceiving a truth differs from knowing why it holds (Proclus against the Epicureans)","Greek geometers gave one case, often the hardest, and left the rest","Euclid often lists corresponding points out of order","A square is 'described on' a side, like multiplying a number by itself"],"terms":[{"t":"paraplērōmata","d":"Complements: figures put in to fill up the spaces about a diameter."},{"t":"anagraphein apo","d":"'To describe on': the verb for drawing a square on a given side."}]},{"i":842,"j":938,"loc":"Book II, Definitions; Propositions 1–6","title":"Rectangles, gnomons and divided lines","summary":"Book II defines a rectangle as contained by the two lines about its right angle, and a gnomon as one parallelogram about a diameter together with the two complements. Propositions 1–3 show that rectangles on a divided line add up: the rectangle on an undivided line and a divided one equals the sum of the rectangles on its parts. Proposition 4 proves that the square on a line cut anywhere equals the squares on the two parts plus twice their rectangle. Propositions 5 and 6 treat a line cut equally and unequally, or bisected and extended, showing by means of a gnomon that a rectangle plus a square equals the square on the half or on the half plus the added part.","ideas":["Areas of rectangles and squares are added and compared as figures","The square on a whole equals the squares on its parts plus twice their rectangle (II.4)","The gnomon is the key figure in II.5–6","II.5 and II.6 are later used to solve equations of area"],"terms":[{"t":"gnōmōn","d":"An L-shaped figure: a parallelogram about the diameter together with its two complements."},{"t":"rectangle contained by","d":"The rectangular parallelogram whose sides about the right angle are the two given straight lines."}]},{"i":939,"j":1067,"loc":"Book II, Propositions 7–11","title":"Further identities and cutting in extreme ratio","summary":"Proposition 7 shows that the squares on a whole line and one part equal twice their rectangle plus the square on the other part. Proposition 8 proves that four times the rectangle of the whole and one part, plus the square on the other part, equals the square on the whole and that part taken as one line. Propositions 9 and 10 use isosceles right triangles and I.47 to show that the squares on the unequal parts are double the squares on the half and on the line between the cuts. Proposition 11 cuts a given line so that the rectangle on the whole and one part equals the square on the other part, using II.6 and I.47.","ideas":["Sums of squares are compared by doubling, via half-right angles","II.8 builds a figure 'drawn double' of eight equal areas","II.11 divides a line so that a rectangle equals a square","Earlier propositions of Book II feed later ones"],"terms":[{"t":"II.11 section","d":"The division of a line so that the whole-by-part rectangle equals the square on the other part; later called extreme and mean ratio."}]},{"i":1068,"j":1115,"loc":"Book II, Propositions 12–14; Endnotes","title":"Obtuse and acute triangles; squaring a figure","summary":"Proposition 12 proves that in an obtuse-angled triangle the square on the side opposite the obtuse angle exceeds the squares on the other two sides by twice a rectangle formed with the perpendicular's projection. Proposition 13 gives the corresponding deficit for the side opposite an acute angle. Proposition 14 constructs a square equal to any given rectilineal figure: a parallelogram equal to it is made by I.45 and then turned into a square using a semicircle and II.5. Heath's brief endnotes explain his phrase 'the rectangle A, BC', the Greek idiom for 'twice the rectangle', and the gnomon's lettering.","ideas":["II.12–13 extend I.47 to obtuse and acute triangles","Any rectilineal figure can be squared (II.14)","The square is found as a mean in a semicircle","Greek says 'the rectangle twice contained' for twice the rectangle"],"terms":[{"t":"rectilineal figure","d":"A figure bounded by straight lines."}]},{"i":1116,"j":1238,"loc":"Book III, Definitions; Propositions 1–7","title":"Circle definitions, centres and chords","summary":"Book III defines equal circles, tangency, distance of chords from the centre, segments, angles of and in segments, sectors and similar segments. Proposition 1 finds the centre of a circle by bisecting a chord at right angles, with a porism that the centre lies on any perpendicular bisector of a chord. Propositions 2–4 show a chord falls within the circle, that a diameter bisecting a chord cuts it at right angles and conversely, and that two chords not through the centre do not bisect each other. Propositions 5 and 6 prove that circles which cut or touch cannot share a centre; III.7 orders the lines from a point on a diameter to the circumference, from greatest to least.","ideas":["The centre lies on the perpendicular bisector of any chord","A diameter and a chord: bisecting and perpendicular go together","Intersecting or touching circles have different centres","From a non-central point, only two equal lines reach the circle"],"terms":[{"t":"segment (tmēma) of a circle","d":"The figure bounded by a straight line and a circumference of a circle."},{"t":"angle in a segment","d":"The angle at a point on the arc formed by lines joined to the ends of the segment's base."},{"t":"to touch (ephaptesthai)","d":"To meet a circle and, being produced, not cut it."}]},{"i":1239,"j":1340,"loc":"Book III, Propositions 8–13","title":"Lines from outside points; meeting circles","summary":"Proposition 8 orders the straight lines drawn from a point outside a circle: of those reaching the concave circumference the one through the centre is greatest, and of those reaching the convex side the one toward the diameter is least, with only two equal lines symmetric about it. Proposition 9 shows that a point from which more than two equal lines reach the circle is its centre. Proposition 10 proves a circle cannot cut another at more than two points. Propositions 11 and 12 prove that the line joining the centres of touching circles passes through the point of contact, and III.13 that circles touch at only one point.","ideas":["Distances from an outside point increase toward the line through the centre","Three equal radii fix the centre (III.9)","Two circles meet in at most two points","The line of centres passes through the point of contact"],"terms":[{"t":"concave / convex circumference","d":"The far and near sides of the circle as seen from an outside point."}]},{"i":1341,"j":1442,"loc":"Book III, Propositions 14–19","title":"Chord distances, tangents and the horn angle","summary":"Proposition 14 proves equal chords equally distant from the centre, and conversely; III.15 shows the diameter is the greatest chord and nearer chords are longer. Proposition 16 proves that the line at right angles to a diameter at its end falls outside the circle and that no straight line can be put between it and the circumference; the angle of the semicircle is greater, and the remaining angle less, than any acute rectilineal angle. Its porism is that such a line touches the circle. Proposition 17 draws a tangent from a given point, and III.18–19 prove the radius to the point of contact perpendicular to the tangent and the centre on the perpendicular at the contact.","ideas":["Chord length and distance from centre correspond (III.14–15)","The tangent is perpendicular to the radius","The angle between circle and tangent is less than any rectilineal angle","Constructing a tangent from an outside point"],"terms":[{"t":"angle of the semicircle","d":"The mixed angle between a diameter and the circumference at its end."},{"t":"hē ephaptomenē (the tangent)","d":"The straight line that touches a circle."}]},{"i":1443,"j":1556,"loc":"Book III, Propositions 20–29","title":"Angles in circles, segments and equal arcs","summary":"Proposition 20 proves the angle at the centre double the angle at the circumference on the same arc. From it follow III.21, that angles in the same segment are equal, and III.22, that opposite angles of a quadrilateral in a circle equal two right angles. Propositions 23–24 show similar segments on the same line cannot be unequal, and similar segments on equal lines are equal; III.25 completes a circle from a given segment, distinguishing segments less than, equal to and greater than a semicircle. Propositions 26–29 link equal angles, equal arcs and equal chords in equal circles.","ideas":["Central angle is double the inscribed angle (III.20)","Angles in the same segment are equal","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral make two right angles","In equal circles, equal angles, arcs and chords imply one another"],"terms":[{"t":"periphereia (circumference)","d":"An arc of a circle, or the whole boundary of it."},{"t":"similar segments","d":"Segments of circles that admit equal angles."}]},{"i":1557,"j":1646,"loc":"Book III, Propositions 30–34","title":"Angle in a semicircle and alternate segments","summary":"Proposition 30 bisects a given arc. Proposition 31 proves that the angle in a semicircle is right, the angle in a greater segment is acute and in a lesser one obtuse; the angle of the greater segment is greater than a right angle, that of the lesser less. Proposition 32 proves that the angle between a tangent and a chord from the point of contact equals the angle in the alternate segment. Propositions 33 and 34 use this to describe on a given line, and to cut off from a given circle, a segment admitting a given angle, with acute, right and obtuse cases treated separately.","ideas":["The angle in a semicircle is right (III.31)","Tangent-chord angle equals the angle in the alternate segment (III.32)","Constructing a segment to hold a given angle","Separate figures for acute, right and obtuse angles"],"terms":[{"t":"alternate segment","d":"The segment on the other side of the chord from the angle it makes with the tangent."}]},{"i":1647,"j":1717,"loc":"Book III, Propositions 35–37; Endnotes","title":"Intersecting chords, tangent and secant","summary":"Proposition 35 proves that when two chords cut each other in a circle, the rectangle on the parts of one equals the rectangle on the parts of the other, using II.5 and I.47. Proposition 36 proves that from a point outside, the rectangle on a whole secant and its outside part equals the square on the tangent, using II.6. Proposition 37 proves the converse: a line from the point whose square equals that rectangle touches the circle. Heath's endnotes to Book III explain Greek phrasing, Euclid's ordering of letters, his slip of 'nearer to the diameter' in III.15, and technical words such as the verb for 'inflecting' a line.","ideas":["Products of chord segments are equal (III.35)","Square on the tangent equals secant times outer part (III.36)","The converse identifies a tangent (III.37)","Book II's area results serve circle geometry"],"terms":[{"t":"keklasthai (to be inflected)","d":"The technical term for drawing a broken straight line from one point to a curve and back to another."}]},{"i":1718,"j":1793,"loc":"Book IV, Definitions 1–7; Propositions 1–5","title":"Inscribing and circumscribing triangles and circles","summary":"Book IV opens with seven definitions of what it means for one figure to be inscribed in or circumscribed about another, and for a straight line to be fitted into a circle. Proposition 1 fits into a circle a chord equal to a given line not longer than the diameter. Propositions 2 and 3 inscribe in, and circumscribe about, a given circle a triangle equiangular with a given triangle, using tangents and the angle in the alternate segment. Proposition 4 inscribes a circle in a triangle by bisecting two of its angles; Proposition 5 circumscribes a circle about a triangle by drawing perpendiculars from the midpoints of two sides. A closing remark notes that the circle's centre falls inside, on, or outside the triangle as the opposite angle is acute, right, or obtuse.","ideas":["Inscribed and circumscribed figures defined by where angles and sides fall","Tangent and alternate-segment angle used to copy a triangle's angles into a circle","Angle bisectors meet at the centre of the inscribed circle","Perpendiculars from midpoints of sides meet at the centre of the circumscribed circle"],"terms":[{"t":"inscribed / circumscribed","d":"A figure is inscribed when its angles lie on the other figure's sides or circumference, circumscribed when its sides pass through or touch the other's angles or circumference."},{"t":"fitted into a circle","d":"Said of a straight line whose two extremities both lie on the circumference."}]},{"i":1794,"j":1876,"loc":"Book IV, Propositions 6–10","title":"Squares in circles and the special isosceles triangle","summary":"Propositions 6 and 7 inscribe a square in a circle and circumscribe one about it, using two diameters at right angles and, for the outer square, the tangents at their ends. Propositions 8 and 9 do the reverse, inscribing a circle in a given square and circumscribing one about it. Proposition 10 constructs an isosceles triangle each of whose base angles is double the vertex angle. Euclid cuts a line as in II. 11, fits a chord into a circle, and uses the tangent criterion of III. 37 and the alternate-segment theorem to show the required angle relations. This triangle is the tool for the pentagon constructions that follow.","ideas":["Perpendicular diameters give an inscribed square; tangents at their ends give a circumscribed one","A square's diagonals and midlines locate the centre of its circles","An isosceles triangle with base angles double the vertex angle is built from the section of II. 11","The construction prepares the regular pentagon"],"terms":[{"t":"Q. E. F.","d":"Quod erat faciendum, 'which was to be done', closing a construction (problem) rather than a theorem."}]},{"i":1877,"j":2004,"loc":"Book IV, Propositions 11–16 and Endnotes","title":"Regular pentagon, hexagon and fifteen-sided figure","summary":"Proposition 11 inscribes an equilateral and equiangular pentagon in a circle: a triangle like that of IV. 10 is inscribed and its base angles bisected, giving five equal arcs. Propositions 12–14 circumscribe such a pentagon about a circle and inscribe and circumscribe a circle in and about a given regular pentagon. Proposition 15 inscribes a regular hexagon, and its porism notes that the hexagon's side equals the radius, with the outer hexagon and its circles made as for the pentagon. Proposition 16 obtains the regular fifteen-sided figure by combining the arcs of an inscribed triangle and pentagon: one-third less one-fifth of the circle leaves two fifteenths, which are bisected. The translator's endnotes comment on Greek idioms, including Euclid's loose phrase 'distance one of the points'.","ideas":["Bisecting the base angles of the 'double-angle' triangle yields five equal arcs","Equal arcs give equal chords and equal angles, so the polygon is regular","The side of the inscribed hexagon equals the radius","The 15-gon comes from the difference between the triangle's and pentagon's arcs"],"terms":[{"t":"porism","d":"A corollary that follows directly from what a proposition has proved."},{"t":"equilateral and equiangular","d":"Euclid's description of what is now called a regular polygon: all sides equal and all angles equal."}]},{"i":2005,"j":2058,"loc":"Book V, Definitions 1–18; Propositions 1–4","title":"The theory of ratio and proportion defined","summary":"Book V begins the general theory of magnitudes in ratio. It defines part and multiple, ratio as a relation in size between magnitudes of the same kind, and magnitudes having a ratio when each can be multiplied to exceed the other. Definition 5 states sameness of ratio by equimultiples: the multiples of first and third alike exceed, equal, or fall short of those of second and fourth. Definition 7 defines a greater ratio, and further definitions name duplicate and triplicate ratio and the operations alternate, inverse, componendo, separando, convertendo, ex aequali and perturbed proportion. Propositions 1–4 prove basic facts about equimultiples, ending with the result that equimultiples of proportional magnitudes are themselves proportional.","ideas":["Ratio is a size relation between magnitudes of one kind","Magnitudes have a ratio only if multiples of each can exceed the other","Same ratio is defined by comparing all equimultiples","Equimultiples of proportionals stay proportional (V. 4)"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (ratio)","d":"A sort of relation in respect of size between two magnitudes of the same kind."},{"t":"equimultiples","d":"Magnitudes that are the same multiple of their respective magnitudes, the basis of the definition of same ratio."},{"t":"duplicate ratio","d":"The ratio of the first to the third of three proportional magnitudes, compared with that of the first to the second."}]},{"i":2059,"j":2134,"loc":"Book V, Propositions 5–8","title":"Remainders of multiples and comparing unequal ratios","summary":"Propositions 5 and 6 show that if a whole is the same multiple of a whole that a subtracted part is of a subtracted part, the remainders follow the same multiple, and treat remainders of equimultiples. Proposition 7 proves that equal magnitudes have the same ratio to a given magnitude and it to them, with a porism that proportionals are also proportional inversely. Proposition 8 proves that of unequal magnitudes the greater has the greater ratio to a given magnitude, and the given magnitude has a greater ratio to the less. The proof uses Definition 4 to find a multiple exceeding the given magnitude and works through two cases by Definition 7.","ideas":["Subtraction preserves the multiple relation between wholes and parts","Equal magnitudes have equal ratios to the same thing","A greater magnitude has the greater ratio to a fixed magnitude","Inverse proportion follows as a porism"],"terms":[{"t":"inversely","d":"Taking the consequent as antecedent and the antecedent as consequent (V. Def. 13)."}]},{"i":2135,"j":2218,"loc":"Book V, Propositions 9–16","title":"Equality, transitivity and alternation of ratios","summary":"Propositions 9 and 10 give the converses of 7 and 8: magnitudes with the same ratio to the same are equal, and the one with the greater ratio is greater. Proposition 11 proves that ratios the same with the same ratio are the same with one another. Proposition 12 shows that, in a run of proportionals, one antecedent is to one consequent as all antecedents to all consequents. Propositions 13 and 14 compare a proportion with a greater ratio and relate the sizes of first and third to second and fourth. Proposition 15 shows parts have the same ratio as equal multiples of them, and Proposition 16 proves that four proportionals are also proportional alternately.","ideas":["Same ratio to the same magnitude forces equality","Sameness of ratio is transitive (V. 11)","Sum of antecedents to sum of consequents keeps the ratio (V. 12)","Alternation: if A:B = C:D then A:C = B:D (V. 16)"],"terms":[{"t":"alternately (enallax)","d":"Taking antecedent to antecedent and consequent to consequent (V. Def. 12)."},{"t":"antecedent / consequent","d":"The first and second terms of a ratio."}]},{"i":2219,"j":2290,"loc":"Book V, Propositions 17–21","title":"Componendo, separando, and the ex aequali lemmas","summary":"Proposition 17 proves that magnitudes proportional componendo are also proportional separando, and Proposition 18 proves the converse by reductio, using V. 14. Proposition 19 shows that if whole is to whole as part subtracted to part subtracted, the remainder is to the remainder as whole to whole; its porism adds that proportion componendo gives proportion convertendo. Propositions 20 and 21 are lemmas for two sets of three magnitudes in the same ratios two by two, in ordinary and in perturbed order. They show that if the first exceeds, equals, or falls short of the third, the fourth does the same to the sixth.","ideas":["Componendo and separando are interchangeable (V. 17–18)","Remainders keep the ratio of wholes (V. 19)","Convertendo follows as a porism","Comparison lemmas prepare the ex aequali theorems"],"terms":[{"t":"componendo / separando","d":"Taking antecedent plus consequent to consequent, or the excess of antecedent over consequent to consequent (V. Defs. 14–15)."},{"t":"perturbed proportion","d":"Two sets of three magnitudes whose ratios correspond in crossed rather than direct order (V. Def. 18)."}]},{"i":2291,"j":2358,"loc":"Book V, Propositions 22–25 and Endnotes","title":"Ratio ex aequali and the end of Book V","summary":"Proposition 22 proves that magnitudes in the same ratio two by two are also in the same ratio ex aequali, for any number of terms, using equimultiples and V. 20. Proposition 23 proves the same for perturbed proportion, with V. 21. Proposition 24 shows that if first is to second as third to fourth, and fifth to second as sixth to fourth, then first plus fifth is to second as third plus sixth to fourth. Proposition 25 proves that of four proportional magnitudes, the greatest and least together exceed the other two. The endnotes explain a construction phrase in V. 5 and why the translator supplies words missing from the terse Greek of proportions.","ideas":["Ex aequali: from A:B = D:E and B:C = E:F follows A:C = D:F","The result also holds for perturbed proportion","Adding magnitudes with the same ratio to a common term","Greatest plus least of four proportionals exceeds the middle two"],"terms":[{"t":"ex aequali (di' isou)","d":"Taking the extreme terms of a chain of ratios, the intermediate terms being removed (V. Def. 17)."}]},{"i":2359,"j":2438,"loc":"Book VI, Definitions 1–5; Propositions 1–4","title":"Similar figures and proportional sides of triangles","summary":"Book VI applies the theory of proportion to plane figures. It defines similar rectilineal figures, cutting a line in extreme and mean ratio, and the height of a figure. Proposition 1 proves that triangles and parallelograms of the same height are to one another as their bases, using the equimultiple definition of Book V. Proposition 2 shows that a line parallel to one side of a triangle cuts the other sides proportionally, and conversely. Proposition 3 proves that the bisector of a triangle's angle cuts the base in the ratio of the other sides, and conversely. Proposition 4 proves that equiangular triangles have the sides about their equal angles proportional.","ideas":["Similar figures: equal angles and proportional sides about them","Same height means area is proportional to base (VI. 1)","A parallel to one side divides the other two proportionally","The angle bisector divides the base in the ratio of the sides"],"terms":[{"t":"similar rectilineal figures","d":"Figures with their angles severally equal and the sides about the equal angles proportional (VI. Def. 1)."},{"t":"extreme and mean ratio","d":"A line is so cut when the whole is to the greater segment as the greater segment is to the less."}]},{"i":2439,"j":2521,"loc":"Book VI, Propositions 5–8","title":"Conditions for similar triangles; right-triangle similarity","summary":"Proposition 5 proves that triangles with proportional sides are equiangular, by building a copy of one triangle's angles on the other's side and applying I. 8. Proposition 6 shows that one equal angle with the sides about it proportional also makes triangles equiangular. Proposition 7 treats one equal angle with the sides about other angles proportional, which suffices when the remaining angles are both less, or both not less, than a right angle. Proposition 8 proves that the perpendicular from the right angle of a right triangle to the hypotenuse divides it into two triangles similar to the whole and to each other. Its porism states that this perpendicular is a mean proportional between the segments of the base.","ideas":["Proportional sides guarantee equal angles (VI. 5)","One equal angle with proportional adjacent sides also suffices (VI. 6)","The ambiguous case needs a condition on the remaining angles (VI. 7)","The altitude of a right triangle is the mean proportional of the base segments"],"terms":[{"t":"mean proportional","d":"A line B such that A is to B as B is to C."}]},{"i":2522,"j":2614,"loc":"Book VI, Propositions 9–17","title":"Proportional constructions and reciprocal sides","summary":"Propositions 9 and 10 cut off a prescribed part of a line and cut an uncut line in the same ratios as a given cut line, using parallels. Propositions 11–13 find a third proportional to two lines, a fourth proportional to three, and a mean proportional between two, the last with a semicircle. Proposition 14 proves that in equal and equiangular parallelograms the sides about the equal angles are reciprocally proportional, and conversely; Proposition 15 proves the same for triangles with one angle equal. Propositions 16 and 17 deduce that four lines are proportional exactly when the rectangle of the extremes equals that of the means, and three lines when the rectangle of the extremes equals the square on the mean.","ideas":["Parallel lines divide a line into prescribed parts","Third, fourth and mean proportionals can be constructed","Equal equiangular figures have reciprocally proportional sides","Proportion of lines equals equality of rectangles (VI. 16–17)"],"terms":[{"t":"reciprocally proportional","d":"Sides about equal angles in which a side of the first is to a side of the second as the other side of the second is to the other side of the first."},{"t":"third proportional","d":"A line C such that A is to B as B is to C."}]},{"i":2615,"j":2711,"loc":"Book VI, Propositions 18–22","title":"Similar figures and the duplicate ratio of sides","summary":"Proposition 18 constructs on a given line a rectilineal figure similar and similarly situated to a given one, by dividing it into triangles. Proposition 19 proves that similar triangles are to one another in the duplicate ratio of corresponding sides, using a third proportional and VI. 15. Its porism states that if three lines are proportional, the first is to the third as a figure on the first to a similar figure on the second. Proposition 20 extends this to polygons, which divide into similar triangles proportional to the wholes. Proposition 21 shows that figures similar to the same figure are similar to one another, and Proposition 22 that similar figures on proportional lines are proportional, and conversely.","ideas":["A figure similar to a given one can be built on any line","Areas of similar figures are in the duplicate ratio of their sides","Similar polygons split into correspondingly similar triangles","Similarity is transitive"],"terms":[{"t":"similarly situated","d":"Placed on corresponding sides so that the matching angles and sides correspond in order."},{"t":"duplicate ratio","d":"The ratio compounded of a ratio with itself; here, the ratio of similar areas compared with that of their sides."}]},{"i":2712,"j":2792,"loc":"Book VI, Propositions 23–27","title":"Compound ratio and parallelograms about the diameter","summary":"Proposition 23 proves that equiangular parallelograms have the ratio compounded of the ratios of their sides, using auxiliary lines K, L, M in those ratios. Proposition 24 shows that parallelograms about the diameter of a parallelogram are similar to the whole and to each other. Proposition 25 constructs one figure similar to a given rectilineal figure and equal to another, using a mean proportional. Proposition 26 proves that a parallelogram similar to the whole and sharing an angle with it lies about the same diameter. Proposition 27 shows that, of parallelograms applied to a line and falling short by figures similar to one on its half, the greatest is the one applied to the half.","ideas":["Areas of equiangular parallelograms compound the ratios of the sides","Parallelograms on a common diagonal are similar to the whole","A figure can be made similar to one figure and equal to another","The maximal deficient parallelogram is applied to half the line"],"terms":[{"t":"compounded ratio","d":"The ratio K to M formed from the ratios K to L and L to M."},{"t":"diameter (of a parallelogram)","d":"Euclid's word for the diagonal."}]},{"i":2793,"j":2909,"loc":"Book VI, Propositions 28–33 and Endnotes","title":"Application of areas and extreme and mean ratio","summary":"Proposition 28 applies to a given line a parallelogram equal to a given figure and falling short by a parallelogram similar to a given one, provided the figure is not greater than the parallelogram on half the line. Proposition 29 solves the corresponding problem in which the parallelogram exceeds by such a figure. Proposition 30 uses VI. 29 to cut a line in extreme and mean ratio. Proposition 31 generalizes the theorem of the right triangle: the figure on the hypotenuse equals the similar figures on the other two sides. Proposition 32 gives a condition for two triangles' sides to lie in a straight line, and Proposition 33 proves that in equal circles angles are as the arcs they stand on. The endnotes discuss the Greek of 'height', 'any angle', homologous figures and compound ratio.","ideas":["Parallelograms applied with a defect, subject to a limit (VI. 28)","Parallelograms applied with an excess (VI. 29)","Extreme and mean section constructed from VI. 29","Similar figures on the sides of a right triangle add up (VI. 31)"],"terms":[{"t":"application of areas","d":"Constructing on a given line a parallelogram equal to a given figure, exactly, or falling short or exceeding by a figure of given shape."},{"t":"gnomon","d":"The L-shaped figure left when a smaller parallelogram about the diameter is removed from a larger one."}]},{"i":2910,"j":2998,"loc":"Book VII, Definitions 1–22; Propositions 1–3","title":"Definitions of number and the greatest common measure","summary":"Book VII turns to arithmetic. It defines the unit, number as a multitude of units, part and parts, even and odd, prime and composite numbers, and multiplication. It also defines plane, solid, square and cube numbers, numerical proportion, similar numbers and perfect numbers. Proposition 1 shows that if repeated subtraction of the less from the greater leaves only a unit, the two numbers are prime to one another. Proposition 2 uses the same procedure to find the greatest common measure of two numbers not prime to one another; its porism states that any common measure of two numbers measures their greatest common measure. Proposition 3 extends the procedure to three numbers.","ideas":["A number is a multitude composed of units","A prime is measured by the unit alone","Repeated subtraction tests primality to one another and finds the greatest common measure","Every common measure divides the greatest common measure"],"terms":[{"t":"monas (unit)","d":"That by virtue of which each existing thing is called one."},{"t":"prime number","d":"A number measured by a unit alone."},{"t":"perfect number","d":"A number equal to the sum of its own parts."}]},{"i":2999,"j":3100,"loc":"Book VII, Propositions 4–15","title":"Parts, parts, and proportion among numbers","summary":"Proposition 4 proves that any smaller number is either a part or parts of a larger one. Propositions 5–8 show that a part or parts of numbers are preserved when numbers are added or subtracted. Propositions 9 and 10 prove the alternate forms of these relations, on which numerical alternation depends. Propositions 11–14 then give the arithmetical counterparts of Book V: remainders keep the ratio of wholes, sums of antecedents and consequents keep the ratio, four proportional numbers are proportional alternately, and ratios hold ex aequali. Proposition 15 shows that if a unit measures one number as often as another number measures a fourth, then alternately the unit measures the second as often as the first measures the fourth.","ideas":["Every smaller number is a part or parts of a larger","Part and parts survive addition and subtraction","Proportion of numbers rests on Def. 20, not on Book V","Alternation and ex aequali proved for numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"part / parts (meros / merē)","d":"A smaller number is a part of a larger if it measures it, and parts of it if it does not."},{"t":"proportional numbers","d":"Numbers where the first is the same multiple, part or parts of the second that the third is of the fourth (VII. Def. 20)."}]},{"i":3101,"j":3237,"loc":"Book VII, Propositions 16–28","title":"Multiplication, least numbers in a ratio, and primes","summary":"Proposition 16 proves that multiplication is commutative, and Propositions 17–18 that products with a common factor are as the other factors. Proposition 19 shows that four numbers are proportional exactly when the product of the extremes equals that of the means. Propositions 20–22 prove that the least numbers in a given ratio measure all others in that ratio the same number of times, and that numbers prime to one another are the least in their ratio, and conversely. Propositions 23–27 show that primality to one another passes to divisors, products, squares and cubes. Proposition 28 shows that two numbers are prime to one another exactly when their sum is prime to each.","ideas":["A by B equals B by A (VII. 16)","Proportion equals equality of cross-products (VII. 19)","Numbers prime to one another are the least in their ratio","Being prime to a number passes to divisors and products"],"terms":[{"t":"prime to one another","d":"Numbers measured by a unit alone as a common measure (VII. Def. 12)."},{"t":"product","d":"The number produced when one number multiplies another; the translator's rendering of the Greek 'number produced from them'."}]},{"i":3238,"j":3388,"loc":"Book VII, Propositions 29–39 and Endnotes","title":"Prime divisors and the least common multiple","summary":"Proposition 29 shows that a prime is prime to any number it does not measure. Proposition 30 proves that a prime measuring a product measures one of the factors. Propositions 31 and 32 show that every composite number is measured by some prime, since an infinite descending series of numbers is impossible, so that every number is prime or has a prime divisor. Proposition 33 finds the least numbers in the same ratio as given numbers by dividing by their greatest common measure. Propositions 34–36 find the least number measured by two or three given numbers and prove it measures any number they measure. Propositions 37–39 connect measuring numbers with parts 'called by the same name' and find the least number having given parts; endnotes explain the translator's renderings.","ideas":["A prime measuring a product measures a factor (VII. 30)","Every number is prime or measured by a prime","Reduction to least terms via the greatest common measure","The least number measured by given numbers measures all their common multiples"],"terms":[{"t":"least number which they measure","d":"Euclid's least common multiple: the smallest number measured by each of the given numbers."},{"t":"part called by the same name","d":"A part named after a number, as a third is named after three."}]},{"i":3389,"j":3466,"loc":"Book VIII, Propositions 1–4","title":"Least numbers in continued proportion","summary":"Book VIII opens with numbers in continued proportion (geometric series). Proposition 1 proves that if the extremes of such a series are prime to one another, the numbers are the least having that ratio. Proposition 2 shows how to find as many numbers as prescribed in continued proportion, least in a given ratio, by repeated multiplication of the ratio's terms; its porism notes that the extremes of three such least numbers are squares, and of four, cubes. Proposition 3 gives the converse of 1: the extremes of least numbers in continued proportion are prime to one another. Proposition 4 finds the least numbers continuously proportional in several given ratios, using least common multiples.","ideas":["Coprime extremes make a geometric series the least in its ratio, and conversely","Least series are built from powers and products of the ratio's terms A and B","Extremes of a least three-term series are squares, of a four-term series cubes","Several ratios can be chained using the least number measured by two terms"],"terms":[{"t":"continued proportion","d":"A series of numbers in which each is to the next in the same ratio."},{"t":"prime to one another","d":"Numbers measured by no common number except the unit."}]},{"i":3467,"j":3548,"loc":"Book VIII, Propositions 5–10","title":"Compound ratios, measuring, and means with unit","summary":"Proposition 5 proves that plane numbers have to one another the ratio compounded of the ratios of their sides. Propositions 6 and 7 show that in a continued proportion, if the first does not measure the second, no term measures any other; so if the first measures the last, it measures the second. Proposition 8 proves that the number of means falling in continued proportion between two numbers is preserved for any two numbers in the same ratio. Propositions 9 and 10 relate the means between two coprime numbers to the means between each of them and the unit, in both directions.","ideas":["Ratio of plane numbers is compounded from the ratios of their sides","Divisibility of first into second governs divisibility throughout a series","The count of interpolated means depends only on the ratio","Means between two coprime numbers match means between each and the unit"],"terms":[{"t":"plane number","d":"A number produced by multiplying two numbers, called its sides."},{"t":"compounded ratio","d":"The ratio obtained by chaining ratios, as G to K from G to H and H to K."},{"t":"mean proportional","d":"A number falling between two others in continued proportion with them."}]},{"i":3549,"j":3622,"loc":"Book VIII, Propositions 11–17","title":"Means between squares and between cubes","summary":"Proposition 11 proves that between two square numbers there is one mean proportional, and that square has to square the ratio duplicate of side to side; Proposition 12 proves the analogue for cubes, with two means and a triplicate ratio. Proposition 13 shows that the squares and cubes of numbers in continued proportion are themselves in continued proportion. Propositions 14 and 15 prove that a square measures a square exactly when side measures side, and likewise for cubes. Propositions 16 and 17 state the corresponding negative versions.","ideas":["One mean between two squares, two means between two cubes","Squares are in the duplicate, cubes in the triplicate, ratio of their sides","Square measures square if and only if side measures side; likewise for cubes"],"terms":[{"t":"duplicate ratio","d":"The ratio of the first to the third of three numbers in continued proportion."},{"t":"triplicate ratio","d":"The ratio of the first to the fourth of four numbers in continued proportion."}]},{"i":3623,"j":3749,"loc":"Book VIII, Propositions 18–27 and endnotes","title":"Similar plane and solid numbers","summary":"Propositions 18 and 19 prove that one mean falls between two similar plane numbers and two means between two similar solid numbers, with duplicate and triplicate ratios of corresponding sides. Propositions 20 and 21 give the converses: one mean between two numbers makes them similar plane numbers, two means make them similar solid numbers. Propositions 22–25 follow: in a three-term series beginning with a square the third is square, in a four-term series beginning with a cube the fourth is cube, and numbers in the ratio of squares (or cubes) share squareness (or cubeness). Propositions 26 and 27 show similar plane numbers are as square to square and similar solid numbers as cube to cube. Endnotes discuss Greek phrasing and a corrupt passage in Proposition 20.","ideas":["Similarity of plane or solid numbers is equivalent to one or two interpolated means","Squareness and cubeness carry along geometric series","Similar plane numbers are as squares, similar solid numbers as cubes","The editor proposes a corrected reading for VIII. 20"],"terms":[{"t":"similar plane numbers","d":"Plane numbers whose sides are proportional."},{"t":"solid number","d":"A number produced by multiplying three numbers, its sides."},{"t":"empiptein (fall in)","d":"The Greek verb for means that 'fall' between numbers, that is, can be interpolated."}]},{"i":3750,"j":3869,"loc":"Book IX, Propositions 1–10","title":"Products of squares and cubes; series from unit","summary":"Book IX begins by proving that the product of two similar plane numbers is square and, conversely, that numbers whose product is square are similar plane numbers. Propositions 3–6 treat cubes: a cube times itself or times a cube is cube, a cube times a number giving a cube means the number is cube, and a number whose square is cube is itself cube. Proposition 7 shows a composite number times any number gives a solid number. Propositions 8–10 study series in continued proportion beginning from the unit: the third and every second term after are squares, the fourth and every third term cubes, the seventh both; if the term after the unit is square (or cube) all are, and if not, only those terms are.","ideas":["Square products arise exactly from similar plane factors","Cubeness is preserved under the operations of IX. 3–6","In a series from the unit, positions determine which terms are squares or cubes","The seventh term from the unit is both square and cube"],"terms":[{"t":"composite number","d":"A number measured by some number other than the unit."}]},{"i":3870,"j":3951,"loc":"Book IX, Propositions 11–14","title":"Divisors of terms in series from unit","summary":"Proposition 11 proves that in a series in continued proportion from the unit the less term measures the greater according to some term of the series; a porism states the matching positions. Proposition 12 shows that any prime measuring the last term also measures the term next to the unit. Proposition 13 proves that if the term after the unit is prime, the greatest term is measured by no numbers except those in the series. Proposition 14 proves that the least number measured by given primes is measured by no other prime.","ideas":["Terms of a series from the unit divide one another by other terms of the series","Every prime divisor of the last term divides the first term after the unit","Powers of a prime have only lower powers of that prime as divisors","The least number measured by given primes has no other prime factor"],"terms":[{"t":"prime number","d":"A number measured by the unit alone."},{"t":"porism","d":"A corollary that follows directly from a proved proposition."}]},{"i":3952,"j":4044,"loc":"Book IX, Propositions 15–20","title":"Proportionals and the infinity of primes","summary":"Proposition 15 proves that for three least numbers in continued proportion, any two added together are prime to the remaining one. Propositions 16 and 17 show that two coprime numbers, or a series with coprime extremes, cannot be continued by another proportional. Propositions 18 and 19 set out when a third proportional to two given numbers, or a fourth proportional to three, can be found, turning on whether the first measures the relevant product. Proposition 20 proves that prime numbers are more than any assigned multitude: adding the unit to the least number measured by given primes yields either a new prime or a number measured by a new prime.","ideas":["Coprimality blocks the extension of a proportion","A third proportional to A, B exists exactly when A measures the square on B","Fourth proportionals are analysed case by case","The proof that the primes outnumber any given multitude"],"terms":[{"t":"third proportional","d":"A number D such that A is to B as B is to D."},{"t":"fourth proportional","d":"A number D such that A is to B as C is to D."}]},{"i":4045,"j":4175,"loc":"Book IX, Propositions 21–36 and endnote","title":"Odd and even numbers; perfect numbers","summary":"Propositions 21–29 prove the rules of odd and even for sums, differences and products: sums of evens are even, an even multitude of odds sums to even, an odd multitude to odd, with corresponding results for subtraction and multiplication. Propositions 30 and 31 show an odd number measuring an even one measures its half, and an odd number prime to a number is prime to its double. Propositions 32–34 classify even-times even and even-times odd numbers by repeated halving. Proposition 35 sums a geometric series, and Proposition 36 proves that if the sum of doubles from the unit is prime, that sum times the last double is a perfect number.","ideas":["Parity rules for addition, subtraction and multiplication","Powers of two are even-times even only; numbers with odd half are even-times odd only","Sum of a geometric series via the excess of the second and last terms","Construction of perfect numbers from a prime sum of doubles"],"terms":[{"t":"perfect number","d":"A number equal to the sum of its own parts."},{"t":"even-times even","d":"A number measured by an even number according to an even number."},{"t":"dyad","d":"The number two, from which doubling begins."}]},{"i":4176,"j":4262,"loc":"Book X, Definitions I; Propositions 1–5","title":"Commensurability and the method of exhaustion","summary":"Book X defines commensurable and incommensurable magnitudes, commensurability in square, and calls an assigned straight line rational, with lines and areas commensurable with it rational and the rest irrational. Proposition 1 proves that repeatedly subtracting more than half from a magnitude eventually leaves less than any given lesser magnitude. Proposition 2 shows that if alternate subtraction never terminates in measuring, the magnitudes are incommensurable. Propositions 3 and 4 find the greatest common measure of two and of three commensurable magnitudes, with porisms. Proposition 5 proves commensurable magnitudes have the ratio of a number to a number.","ideas":["Rational and irrational are defined relative to an assigned line","Repeated halving can make a magnitude smaller than any given one","Non-terminating alternate subtraction signals incommensurability","Commensurable magnitudes stand in numerical ratio"],"terms":[{"t":"commensurable","d":"Measured by a common measure."},{"t":"commensurable in square","d":"Lines whose squares have a common area as measure, though the lines may not."},{"t":"rational (rhētos)","d":"Commensurable, in length or in square, with an assigned straight line."}]},{"i":4263,"j":4361,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 6–13 and lemmas","title":"Numerical ratio and incommensurable lines","summary":"Proposition 6 proves the converse of 5: magnitudes in the ratio of numbers are commensurable, with a porism on constructing lines and figures in a given numerical ratio. Propositions 7 and 8 give the incommensurable counterparts. Proposition 9 shows lines are commensurable in length exactly when their squares are as square number to square number; its porism notes that commensurability in length implies commensurability in square but not conversely. Proposition 10, bracketed in the text, finds lines incommensurable with a given line in length only and in square. Propositions 11–13 show commensurability carries over proportions and through a common magnitude; a lemma finds the square by which one square exceeds another.","ideas":["Commensurability is equivalent to having a numerical ratio","Lines commensurable in length are those whose squares are as square numbers","Commensurability is transitive and passes through proportions","Semicircle construction finds the difference or sum of two squares"],"terms":[{"t":"commensurable in length","d":"Lines having a common linear measure."},{"t":"lemma","d":"An auxiliary result proved for use in a following proposition."}]},{"i":4362,"j":4452,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 14–19 and lemmas","title":"Squares' excess, application, rational rectangles","summary":"Proposition 14 shows that in four proportional lines, if the first's square exceeds the second's by the square on a line commensurable with the first, the same holds for the third and fourth. Propositions 15 and 16 prove that adding commensurable magnitudes keeps the whole commensurable with each part, and incommensurable ones keep it incommensurable. After a lemma on parallelograms deficient by a square, Propositions 17 and 18 link the commensurability of the segments of a line cut by such an application to whether its square exceeds another square by a square commensurable with it. A lemma explains the terminology of rational lines, and Proposition 19 proves the rectangle on rational lines commensurable in length is rational.","ideas":["Commensurability of the 'excess' line is preserved in proportion","Sums of commensurable parts are commensurable with each part","Application of an area deficient by a square tests commensurability of segments","Rectangle on rational lines commensurable in length is rational"],"terms":[{"t":"application of areas","d":"Placing a parallelogram on a given line, here falling short by a square figure."},{"t":"rational in square only","d":"A line commensurable with the assigned rational line in square but not in length."}]},{"i":4453,"j":4537,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 20–26","title":"The medial line and medial areas","summary":"Proposition 20 shows a rational area applied to a rational line gives a rational breadth commensurable in length with it. Proposition 21 proves the rectangle on rational lines commensurable in square only is irrational, and names the side of the square equal to it medial. After a lemma relating a line ratio to a square and rectangle, Proposition 22 shows the square on a medial applied to a rational line gives a breadth rational but incommensurable in length with it. Proposition 23 and its porism show lines and areas commensurable with medials are medial. Propositions 24–26 prove rectangles on medial lines are medial or rational, and that a medial area does not exceed a medial area by a rational area.","ideas":["Introduction of the medial, the first named irrational line","Applying medial squares to rational lines yields lines rational in square only","Commensurability with a medial preserves mediality","A medial area cannot exceed a medial by a rational area"],"terms":[{"t":"medial (mesē)","d":"The side of a square equal to a rectangle on rational lines commensurable in square only."},{"t":"irrational (alogos)","d":"Incommensurable with the assigned rational line or square."}]},{"i":4538,"j":4648,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 27–32 and lemmas","title":"Constructing pairs of rational and medial lines","summary":"Propositions 27 and 28 find medial lines commensurable in square only containing a rational and a medial rectangle respectively. Two lemmas find two square numbers whose sum is square, and two whose sum is not square, using the theorem on a bisected line. Propositions 29 and 30 use these to find rational lines commensurable in square only whose squares differ by the square on a line commensurable, or incommensurable, in length with the greater. Propositions 31 and 32 find the corresponding pairs of medial lines, containing rational or medial rectangles. A closing lemma on the perpendicular in a right-angled triangle proves equalities of rectangles and squares used next.","ideas":["Medial pairs containing rational or medial rectangles can be constructed","Number lemmas produce square numbers with square and non-square sums","Rational and medial pairs are classified by the commensurability of their squares' difference","Perpendicular in a right triangle yields mean-proportional rectangles"],"terms":[{"t":"square number","d":"A number made by multiplying a number by itself."},{"t":"convertendo","d":"Inferring from A:B = C:D that A is to A−B as C is to C−D."}]},{"i":4649,"j":4707,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 33–38","title":"Binomial and bimedial straight lines","summary":"Propositions 33–35 find pairs of lines incommensurable in square with, respectively, rational sum of squares and medial rectangle, medial sum and rational rectangle, and medial sum and medial rectangle incommensurable with the sum. Each uses a semicircle, a deficient application and the preceding lemma. Proposition 36 proves the sum of two rational lines commensurable in square only is irrational and names it binomial. Propositions 37 and 38 prove the sums of two medial lines commensurable in square only, containing a rational or a medial rectangle, are irrational, named the first and second bimedial.","ideas":["Three constructions of lines incommensurable in square with prescribed properties","Binomial: sum of two rational lines commensurable in square only","First and second bimedial: sums of medial pairs","Irrationality is shown via the square on the whole, by Proposition II. 4"],"terms":[{"t":"binomial (ek duo onomatōn)","d":"Sum of two rational lines commensurable in square only."},{"t":"first bimedial","d":"Sum of two medial lines commensurable in square only containing a rational rectangle."},{"t":"second bimedial","d":"Sum of two such medial lines containing a medial rectangle."}]},{"i":4708,"j":4801,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 39–47","title":"Major and related irrationals; unique division","summary":"Propositions 39–41 prove that the sums of the pairs found in 33–35 are irrational, naming them the major, the side of a rational plus a medial area, and the side of the sum of two medial areas. A lemma shows that the nearer a point of division is to the midpoint, the smaller the sum of the squares on the segments. Propositions 42–47 then prove that each of the six irrationals so far named, binomial, first and second bimedial, major, side of a rational plus a medial, and side of two medials, is divided into its terms at one point only. The proofs rely chiefly on X. 26 and, for the last, on the uniqueness of the binomial's division.","ideas":["Three further irrationals defined by sums of lines incommensurable in square","The sum of squares on segments decreases toward the midpoint","Each of the six additive irrationals divides into its terms uniquely","Uniqueness rests on a medial not exceeding a medial by a rational"],"terms":[{"t":"major (meizōn)","d":"Sum of two lines incommensurable in square with rational sum of squares and medial rectangle."},{"t":"side of a rational plus a medial area","d":"Sum of two lines incommensurable in square with medial sum of squares and rational rectangle."},{"t":"side of the sum of two medial areas","d":"Sum of two such lines with medial sum, medial rectangle, the two incommensurable."}]},{"i":4802,"j":4902,"loc":"Book X, Definitions II; Propositions 48–53","title":"Six kinds of binomial defined and found","summary":"Euclid's second set of definitions in Book X sorts binomial straight lines into six orders, according to whether the square on the greater term exceeds that on the lesser by the square on a line commensurable or incommensurable in length with the greater term, and which term (greater, lesser, or neither) is commensurable in length with a given rational line. Propositions 48–53 then construct each of the six in turn. Each construction chooses numbers with or without the ratio of square to square, uses X. 6 to make squares on lines stand in those numerical ratios, and applies X. 9 to establish commensurability or incommensurability in length. Every proof ends by checking the defining conditions for its order.","ideas":["Binomials fall into six orders under two tests: how the squares on the terms differ, and which term is commensurable with the rational line","Square numbers in the chosen ratios control whether lines turn out commensurable in length","The third and sixth binomials need an extra non-square number D so that neither term is commensurable with the rational line"],"terms":[{"t":"binomial (ek duo onomatōn)","d":"A straight line made of two rational terms commensurable in square only, added together."},{"t":"commensurable in square only","d":"Lines whose squares have a common measure although the lines themselves have none."},{"t":"convertendo","d":"Inferring from a:b = c:d that a:(a−b) = c:(c−d)."}]},{"i":4903,"j":4961,"loc":"Book X, Lemma; Propositions 54–55","title":"Square roots of rational-times-binomial areas","summary":"A lemma shows that when two squares are set corner to corner and the figure completed, the whole is a square, and the rectangle between them is a mean proportional between the two squares. Proposition 54 uses it to prove that the side of an area contained by a rational line and a first binomial is a binomial. The first binomial's lesser term is bisected, a rectangle equal to the square on half of it is applied to the greater term (X. 17), and the area is rebuilt as a square whose two parts are rational and commensurable in square only. Proposition 55 repeats the construction for the second binomial and shows the side is a first bimedial, since the parts come out medial while the rectangle they contain is rational.","ideas":["Two squares placed corner to corner complete a larger square with mean-proportional complements","Applying a rectangle deficient by a square splits the greater term into parts that build the side","Rational line times first binomial gives a binomial side; times second binomial gives a first bimedial side"],"terms":[{"t":"\"side\" of an area","d":"Heath's short form for the side of the square equal to that area."},{"t":"first bimedial","d":"The sum of two medial lines commensurable in square only that contain a rational rectangle (X. 37)."},{"t":"mean proportional","d":"A middle term b with a:b = b:c."}]},{"i":4962,"j":5014,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 56–59; Lemma","title":"Sides of areas with the third to sixth binomials","summary":"With the same construction as in Proposition 54, Euclid finds the side of an area contained by a rational line and each remaining binomial. The third binomial gives a second bimedial (the rectangle of the parts is medial); the fourth gives the major line (parts incommensurable in square, sum of squares rational, rectangle medial). The fifth gives the side of a rational plus a medial area (sum of squares medial, rectangle rational), and the sixth gives the side of the sum of two medial areas. A closing lemma proves that when a line is cut unequally, the squares on the parts are greater than twice the rectangle they contain.","ideas":["The six binomial orders correspond one-to-one with the six irrational lines formed by addition","Whether the sum of squares and the rectangle come out rational or medial decides which irrational results","Squares on unequal parts always exceed twice their rectangle (from II. 5 and II. 9)"],"terms":[{"t":"major (meizōn)","d":"Sum of two lines incommensurable in square with rational sum of squares and medial rectangle (X. 39)."},{"t":"medial (mesē)","d":"A line whose square equals a rectangle contained by rational lines commensurable in square only."}]},{"i":5015,"j":5100,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 60–65","title":"Squares on the six irrationals applied to rationals","summary":"Propositions 60–65 reverse the previous results. The square on a binomial, applied to a rational line, produces a first binomial as breadth; the squares on the first bimedial, second bimedial, major, side of a rational plus a medial area, and side of the sum of two medial areas produce the second through sixth binomials respectively. In each proof the applied area is split into the squares on the two parts and twice their rectangle, and the breadths of these pieces are tested by X. 20 and X. 22. X. 17 or X. 18, together with the lemma on the squares exceeding twice the rectangle, then fixes the order of the binomial produced.","ideas":["Applying the square of each additive irrational to a rational line yields a binomial of matching order","A rational area applied to a rational line gives a breadth commensurable with it; a medial area gives one incommensurable","The order of the breadth tracks the kind of irrational, confirming the six types are distinct"],"terms":[{"t":"application of areas (parabolē)","d":"Constructing on a given line a rectangle equal to a given area, whose other side is the breadth."},{"t":"breadth (platos)","d":"The second side produced when an area is applied to a given line."}]},{"i":5101,"j":5161,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 66–70","title":"Lines commensurable with the irrationals keep their type","summary":"Euclid proves that a line commensurable in length with a binomial is a binomial of the same order, and that a line commensurable with a bimedial is a bimedial of the same order. Lines commensurable with a major, with the side of a rational plus a medial area, or with the side of the sum of two medial areas are of the same kind. Each proof cuts the second line in the same ratio as the first (VI. 12) so that its parts are commensurable with the first line's parts. The rationality or mediality of the squares and rectangles then carries over, and X. 14 preserves the commensurability conditions that fix the order.","ideas":["Each type of irrational is closed under commensurability in length","Dividing proportionally carries over every defining property of the parts","The order of binomial or bimedial is also preserved"],"terms":[{"t":"same in order (tē taxei hē autē)","d":"Belonging to the same numbered subclass, e.g. both first binomials."}]},{"i":5162,"j":5210,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 71–72","title":"Adding rational and medial areas","summary":"Proposition 71 shows that the side of a rational area added to a medial area is one of four irrationals: a binomial, first bimedial, major, or side of a rational plus a medial area. Both areas are applied to a rational line, giving a binomial breadth; which of the first, second, fourth or fifth orders it belongs to depends on which area is greater and how the squares on the terms differ. Propositions 54, 55, 57 and 58 then name the side. Proposition 72 shows that two incommensurable medial areas added together give a second bimedial or the side of the sum of two medial areas, through the third and sixth binomials.","ideas":["All six additive irrationals arise from adding rational and medial areas","Case analysis on which area is greater and on the commensurability of the difference of squares","Earlier results on binomial areas are reused to identify each side"],"terms":[{"t":"medial area","d":"An area equal to a rectangle contained by rational lines commensurable in square only."}]},{"i":5211,"j":5265,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 73–78","title":"The six irrationals formed by subtraction","summary":"These propositions define the subtractive counterparts of the six additive irrationals. Subtracting from a rational line a rational line commensurable with it in square only leaves an apotome (73). Subtracting medial lines commensurable in square only that contain a rational or a medial rectangle leaves a first or second apotome of a medial line (74–75). Subtracting lines incommensurable in square under the conditions of the major, of the side of a rational plus medial area, and of the side of two medial areas leaves the minor and the lines that produce with a rational area, or with a medial area, a medial whole (76–78). Each remainder is shown irrational using II. 7 and X. 16: its square is incommensurable with a rational area or with the breadth of an apotome.","ideas":["Apotome is the subtractive twin of the binomial","The square on the remainder equals the squares on the two lines minus twice their rectangle (II. 7)","Six new irrationals are named, one for each additive type"],"terms":[{"t":"apotomē (apotome)","d":"The remainder when a rational line commensurable in square only with a rational whole is cut off from it."},{"t":"minor (elassōn)","d":"The remainder of two lines incommensurable in square with rational sum of squares and medial rectangle (X. 76)."}]},{"i":5266,"j":5330,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 79–84","title":"Each subtractive irrational has only one annex","summary":"Euclid proves that for each of the six subtractive irrationals only one straight line can be annexed that satisfies the defining conditions with the whole. For the apotome (79), two different annexes would make two medial rectangles differ by a rational area, which X. 26 forbids. Propositions 80, 82 and 83 argue the same way for the first apotome of a medial line, the minor, and the line producing with a rational area a medial whole. For the second apotome of a medial line (81) and the line producing with a medial area a medial whole (84), the areas are applied to a rational line and a second annex would give an apotome two annexes, contradicting Proposition 79.","ideas":["Uniqueness of the annex makes each subtractive irrational well-defined","A medial area cannot exceed a medial area by a rational area (X. 26)","Later cases reduce to the uniqueness proved for the apotome"],"terms":[{"t":"annex (prosarmozousa)","d":"The line which, added to an apotome-type line, completes the whole from which it was subtracted."}]},{"i":5331,"j":5437,"loc":"Book X, Definitions III; Propositions 85–90","title":"Six kinds of apotome defined and found","summary":"The third set of definitions sorts apotomes into six orders, in parallel with the binomials. The square on the whole exceeds that on the annex by the square on a line commensurable (first to third) or incommensurable (fourth to sixth) with the whole, and the whole, the annex, or neither is commensurable in length with the rational line. Propositions 85–90 construct each order. Square numbers whose difference is not square are used for the first and second; three numbers not in square ratio for the third and sixth; numbers with no square ratio to their sum for the fourth and fifth. As in Propositions 48–53, X. 6 and X. 9 turn numerical ratios into commensurability of lines.","ideas":["Apotome orders are defined exactly as binomial orders, with whole and annex in place of the two terms","Each order is shown to exist by an explicit construction from chosen numbers","Ex aequali (V. 22) shows that neither line in the third and sixth is commensurable with the rational line"],"terms":[{"t":"first apotome","d":"An apotome whose whole is commensurable in length with the rational line and exceeds the annex in square by a line commensurable with it."},{"t":"ex aequali","d":"Reasoning from two chains of equal ratios to the ratio of their extremes."}]},{"i":5438,"j":5520,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 91–93","title":"Sides of areas with first to third apotomes","summary":"Proposition 91 proves that the side of an area contained by a rational line and a first apotome is an apotome. The annex is bisected, a rectangle equal to the square on its half is applied to the whole (X. 17), and two squares about the same diameter (VI. 26) are built. The leftover gnomon is subtracted, so the area equals the square on the difference of their sides. Proposition 92 shows the second apotome gives a first apotome of a medial line, the parts being medial with a rational rectangle. Proposition 93 shows the third apotome gives a second apotome of a medial line, the rectangle being medial.","ideas":["The construction mirrors Propositions 54–56, with subtraction of a square in place of addition","Squares about the same diameter and a gnomon express the area as the square on a difference","Rational line times first, second, third apotome gives apotome, first and second apotome of a medial line"],"terms":[{"t":"gnomon","d":"The L-shaped figure left when a smaller parallelogram about the same diameter is removed from a larger one."}]},{"i":5521,"j":5580,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 94–96","title":"Sides of areas with fourth to sixth apotomes","summary":"Continuing the same construction, Euclid shows that an area contained by a rational line and a fourth apotome has as its side the minor line. With a fifth apotome the side is the line that produces with a rational area a medial whole, and with a sixth apotome it is the line that produces with a medial area a medial whole. Here X. 18 divides the whole into incommensurable parts, so the two built squares are incommensurable with each other. The rationality or mediality of the whole area and of the strip on the annex then decides which subtractive irrational the side is.","ideas":["The six apotome orders correspond one-to-one with the six subtractive irrationals","Incommensurable parts make the side's two components incommensurable in square","Rational or medial sum of squares and rectangle identify the type"],"terms":[{"t":"that which produces with a rational area a medial whole","d":"The subtractive counterpart of the side of a rational plus a medial area (X. 77)."}]},{"i":5581,"j":5641,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 97–99","title":"Squares on apotomes applied to rationals","summary":"Reversing Propositions 91–93, Euclid applies the squares of subtractive irrationals to a rational line. The square on an apotome produces a first apotome as breadth, that on a first apotome of a medial line a second apotome, and that on a second apotome of a medial line a third apotome. In each proof the squares on whole and annex are applied, the square on the line itself is removed, and the remainder equals twice the rectangle (II. 7). Halving that strip and using the mean proportional between the squares gives a rectangle equal to a quarter of the square on the remainder, so X. 17 fixes the order.","ideas":["Applying the square of each subtractive irrational to a rational line yields an apotome of matching order","The rectangle of whole and annex is a mean proportional between their squares","Commensurable squares on whole and annex give the first three orders"],"terms":[{"t":"first apotome of a medial straight line","d":"Remainder of medial lines commensurable in square only containing a rational rectangle (X. 74)."}]},{"i":5642,"j":5703,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 100–102","title":"Squares on minor and related lines applied","summary":"The square on a minor line applied to a rational line produces a fourth apotome. The square on the line producing with a rational area a medial whole produces a fifth apotome, and that on the line producing with a medial area a medial whole produces a sixth. The method is that of Propositions 97–99. Since the whole and annex are now incommensurable in square, the applied squares divide the breadth into incommensurable parts, and X. 18 shows the square on the greater exceeds the lesser by a line incommensurable with it. The rationality of the whole area or of the strip then settles the order.","ideas":["Completes the correspondence between subtractive irrationals and apotome orders","Incommensurable squares on whole and annex lead to the fourth to sixth orders","Rational sum of squares gives the fourth; rational rectangle the fifth; neither the sixth"],"terms":[]},{"i":5704,"j":5749,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 103–107","title":"Lines commensurable with subtractive irrationals","summary":"Parallel to Propositions 66–70, Euclid proves that a line commensurable in length with an apotome is an apotome of the same order. A line commensurable with an apotome of a medial line is one of the same order, and lines commensurable with a minor, or with either line producing a medial whole, are of the same kind. The second line is given an annex in the same ratio to it as the first line's annex (VI. 12, V. 12), so whole and annex are commensurable respectively. The commensurability of the squares and rectangles then carries over every defining condition.","ideas":["Each subtractive irrational is closed under commensurability","Proportional annexes preserve rationality, mediality and incommensurability","The order of apotome is also preserved"],"terms":[]},{"i":5750,"j":5813,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 108–112","title":"Subtracting areas; apotome and binomial distinct","summary":"Propositions 108–110 show that subtracting a medial area from a rational one leaves a side that is an apotome or a minor. A rational area from a medial one gives a first apotome of a medial line or the line producing with a rational area a medial whole; a medial area from an incommensurable medial one gives a second apotome of a medial line or the line producing with a medial area a medial whole. Proposition 111 proves that the apotome is not the same as the binomial, since applying the square of one line to a rational would make it both a first apotome and a first binomial, forcing a rational line to be an apotome. Proposition 112 shows that the square on a rational line applied to a binomial gives an apotome of the same order, with terms commensurable and in the same ratio.","ideas":["Subtracting rational and medial areas generates all six subtractive irrationals","Apotome and binomial cannot coincide","A rational square divided by a binomial yields a matching apotome"],"terms":[{"t":"terms (onomata)","d":"The two component lines of a binomial, or whole and annex of an apotome."}]},{"i":5814,"j":5866,"loc":"Book X, Propositions 113–115; Endnotes","title":"Binomial–apotome reciprocity and infinitely many irrationals","summary":"Proposition 113 is the converse of 112: the square on a rational line applied to an apotome gives a binomial of the same order, with terms commensurable and in the same ratio. Proposition 114 shows that a rectangle contained by an apotome and a binomial with proportional, commensurable terms has a rational side. A porism draws the lesson that a rational area can be contained by irrational lines. Proposition 115 closes Book X by showing that from a medial line arise infinitely many irrationals, each new one the side of a rectangle of a rational line and the previous one, none the same as any before. Translator's endnotes follow on readings, the rendering of \"itself,\" the untranslatable term dunatai, eidē as \"types,\" and the meaning of \"side.\"","ideas":["Binomials and apotomes of the same order are reciprocal with respect to a rational square","A rational area can be contained by two irrational lines","Repeated mean proportionals produce an endless series of new irrationals"],"terms":[{"t":"porism","d":"A corollary that follows from a proposition's proof."},{"t":"dunasthai (to be able in square)","d":"Technical Greek verb for a line whose square equals a given area, which Heath renders by paraphrase."}]},{"i":5867,"j":5937,"loc":"Book XI, Definitions 1–28; Propositions 1–4","title":"Solid geometry: definitions and first propositions","summary":"Book XI opens with twenty-eight definitions for solid geometry: the solid, a line or plane at right angles to a plane, inclinations, parallel planes, similar solids, the solid angle, pyramid, prism, sphere, cone, cylinder and the cube, octahedron, icosahedron and dodecahedron. Propositions 1–3 prove that a straight line cannot lie partly in one plane and partly in a higher one, that intersecting lines and every triangle lie in one plane, and that two cutting planes meet in a straight line. Proposition 4 shows that a line at right angles to two intersecting lines at their meeting point is at right angles to the plane through them.","ideas":["Sphere, cone and cylinder are defined by rotating a semicircle, right triangle or rectangle about a fixed side","A solid angle is formed by more than two plane angles meeting at one point","Intersecting lines determine a single plane","Perpendicularity to two lines in a plane gives perpendicularity to the plane"],"terms":[{"t":"plane of reference","d":"The given plane, assumed as base, against which elevated lines and points are measured."},{"t":"solid angle","d":"An angle contained by more than two plane angles not in one plane, meeting at one point."}]},{"i":5938,"j":6040,"loc":"Book XI, Propositions 5–14","title":"Perpendiculars, parallels and planes in space","summary":"Euclid proves that three lines all at right angles to one line at a common point lie in one plane (5), and that two lines at right angles to the same plane are parallel (6). He shows that a line joining points on two parallels lies in their plane (7), that a parallel to a line perpendicular to a plane is also perpendicular to it (8), and that lines parallel to the same line are parallel to each other (9). Lines meeting at an angle that are parallel to two others contain equal angles (10). He then constructs a perpendicular from a point above a plane (11) and from a point in it (12), proves only one such perpendicular is possible on one side (13), and that planes perpendicular to the same line are parallel (14).","ideas":["Parallelism of lines carries over from the plane into space","Perpendiculars to a plane can be dropped and erected, and are unique","A common perpendicular makes two planes parallel"],"terms":[{"t":"parallel planes","d":"Planes which do not meet however far produced (XI Def. 8)."}]},{"i":6041,"j":6122,"loc":"Book XI, Propositions 15–22","title":"Parallel planes and the limits of solid angles","summary":"Proposition 15 shows that planes through two pairs of parallel meeting lines are parallel, and 16 that two parallel planes cut by a third have parallel common sections. Proposition 17 proves that parallel planes cut any two straight lines in the same ratios. Propositions 18–19 show that every plane through a line perpendicular to a plane is itself perpendicular to it, and that the common section of two planes perpendicular to a plane is perpendicular to it. Propositions 20–21 prove that in a solid angle of three plane angles any two exceed the third, and that the plane angles of any solid angle total less than four right angles. Proposition 22 shows that three such angles with equal arms yield three joining lines that can form a triangle.","ideas":["Parallel planes cut lines proportionally","Any two face angles of a three-faced solid angle exceed the third","The face angles of any solid angle sum to less than four right angles"],"terms":[{"t":"common section","d":"The line in which two planes meet one another."}]},{"i":6123,"j":6206,"loc":"Book XI, Propositions 23–26 (with Lemma)","title":"Constructing solid angles; parallelepipeds begin","summary":"Proposition 23 constructs a solid angle from three given plane angles, any two exceeding the third and all less than four right angles, by circumscribing a circle about a triangle of the joining lines and raising a perpendicular at its centre; a lemma shows how to find the square difference needed. Proposition 24 proves that a solid bounded by parallel planes has its opposite faces equal parallelograms. Proposition 25 shows that a parallelepiped cut by a plane parallel to two opposite faces is divided in the ratio of the bases, using the equimultiple definition of proportion. Proposition 26 constructs, at a point on a given line, a solid angle equal to a given one.","ideas":["The conditions of XI.20–21 are sufficient as well as necessary for a solid angle","Opposite faces of a parallelepiped are equal and parallel","Volumes of parallelepipeds on a common height follow the bases (first case)"],"terms":[{"t":"parallelepipedal solid","d":"A solid contained by three pairs of parallel planes, each face a parallelogram."}]},{"i":6207,"j":6282,"loc":"Book XI, Propositions 27–33 (with Porism)","title":"Equal and similar parallelepipeds","summary":"Euclid describes on a given line a parallelepiped similar to a given one (27) and shows that a plane through diagonals of opposite faces bisects a parallelepiped (28). Propositions 29–31 prove that parallelepipeds on the same or equal bases and with the same height are equal, whether or not the ends of their standing edges lie on the same lines or stand at right angles. Proposition 32 concludes that parallelepipeds of the same height are as their bases. Proposition 33 proves that similar parallelepipeds are in the triplicate ratio of corresponding sides, and a porism adds that for four lines in continued proportion, the solid on the first is to the similar solid on the second as the first line is to the fourth.","ideas":["Equal base and equal height give equal parallelepipeds regardless of slant","A diagonal plane halves a parallelepiped into two equal prisms","Similar solids vary as the cube of the ratio of sides"],"terms":[{"t":"triplicate ratio","d":"The ratio of the first to the fourth of four magnitudes in continued proportion (V Def. 10)."}]},{"i":6283,"j":6385,"loc":"Book XI, Propositions 34–39 (with Porism, Endnotes)","title":"Reciprocal proportion, the cube's centre, and prisms","summary":"Proposition 34 proves that equal parallelepipeds have bases reciprocally proportional to heights, and the converse, treating upright and slanting edges in turn. Proposition 35 shows that equal plane angles with equally inclined elevated lines yield equal angles with their projections, and a porism that equal elevated lines then give equal heights. Using this, 36 proves that the solid formed from three proportional lines equals the equiangular equilateral solid on the mean, and 37 that four lines are proportional exactly when similar solids on them are. Proposition 38 shows that the planes bisecting a cube's opposite faces meet in a line bisecting the diameter, and 39 that a prism on a parallelogram equals one of the same height on a triangle half its size. Brief endnotes gloss Greek terms.","ideas":["Equal volume means base and height vary inversely","Proportion among lines matches proportion among similar solids on them","The diameter of a cube and the bisecting planes' section cut each other in half"],"terms":[{"t":"reciprocally proportional","d":"Related so that base is to base as the second height is to the first."}]},{"i":6386,"j":6406,"loc":"Book XII, Historical Note","title":"Eudoxus, Democritus and the method of exhaustion","summary":"The editor's note says the method of exhaustion dominates Book XII and credits it largely to Eudoxus, citing Archimedes' prefaces to On the Sphere and Cylinder and the Quadrature of the Parabola. It argues that the results of XII.2 and XII.7 were known earlier: Eudemus reports Hippocrates of Chios on circles and the squares on diameters, and Archimedes' recovered Method credits Democritus with first stating, without strict proof, that pyramid and cone are a third of prism and cylinder. The note describes Archimedes' mechanical method for the parabolic segment, which he says gives only an indication, not a proof. It then cites Plutarch on Democritus' puzzle about slicing a cone, suggesting he thought of solids as sums of thin parallel sections.","ideas":["Eudoxus gave the rigorous proofs of the pyramid and cone theorems","Democritus stated them first, without rigorous proof","Archimedes separated mechanical discovery from geometrical proof","Democritus' cone puzzle anticipates thinking of solids as sums of slices"],"terms":[{"t":"method of exhaustion","d":"Proof by inscribing figures that leave a remainder smaller than any assigned magnitude, combined with reductio arguments."},{"t":"Method (ephodos) of Archimedes","d":"Archimedes' treatise to Eratosthenes on finding theorems by mechanical balancing, found by Heiberg in 1906."}]},{"i":6407,"j":6480,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 1–3 (with Lemma)","title":"Circles are as squares on their diameters","summary":"Proposition 1 proves that similar polygons inscribed in circles are as the squares on the diameters. Proposition 2 proves that circles themselves are as the squares on their diameters: inscribing a square and repeatedly doubling the sides leaves segments smaller than any given excess (by X.1), so assuming the ratio holds to a lesser or greater area leads to contradiction; a lemma handles the greater case. Proposition 3 divides a triangular pyramid into two equal pyramids similar to the whole and two equal prisms, and shows the prisms exceed half the pyramid.","ideas":["Inscribed polygons can be made to exhaust a circle","Double reductio rules out both a smaller and a larger fourth term","Each subdivision of a pyramid removes more than half of it as prisms"],"terms":[{"t":"X.1 (bisection principle)","d":"If more than half is repeatedly taken from a magnitude, what remains eventually becomes less than any given magnitude."}]},{"i":6481,"j":6547,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 4–7 (with Lemma, Porism)","title":"Pyramids of equal height are as bases","summary":"Proposition 4 shows that when two pyramids of equal height are divided as in XII.3, all the prisms in one are to all those in the other as the bases; a lemma proves the prisms have equal heights. Proposition 5 then proves by exhaustion that triangular pyramids of the same height are as their bases, and 6 extends this to polygonal bases by dividing them into triangles. Proposition 7 divides a triangular prism into three equal pyramids, and its porism states that any pyramid is a third of the prism with the same base and height.","ideas":["Prisms inside pyramids track the ratio of the bases","Exhaustion transfers the ratio from prisms to pyramids","A pyramid is one third of its prism"],"terms":[]},{"i":6548,"j":6608,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 8–10 (with Porism)","title":"Similar pyramids, and the cone as third of cylinder","summary":"Proposition 8 proves that similar triangular pyramids are in the triplicate ratio of corresponding sides, since each is a sixth of a similar parallelepiped; a porism extends this to polygonal bases. Proposition 9 proves that equal pyramids have bases reciprocally proportional to heights, and the converse. Proposition 10 proves by exhaustion that any cone is a third of the cylinder on the same base and with equal height: inscribed prisms and pyramids on many-sided polygons show the cylinder can be neither more nor less than triple the cone.","ideas":["Pyramid volumes inherit the cube law from parallelepipeds","Equal pyramids balance base against height","The cone result follows from the pyramid result by exhaustion"],"terms":[]},{"i":6609,"j":6679,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 11–12","title":"Cones and cylinders: bases and similarity","summary":"Proposition 11 proves that cones and cylinders of the same height are as their bases, by inscribing pyramids on similar polygons and using XII.1–2 and XII.6 in a double reductio. Proposition 12 proves that similar cones and cylinders are in the triplicate ratio of the diameters of their bases. Euclid shows that corresponding pyramids inscribed in similar cones are similar, so they are in the triplicate ratio of sides, and exhaustion carries the ratio to the cones; cylinders follow because each is triple its cone.","ideas":["Equal height makes cones and cylinders vary with the base circles","Similar cones vary as the cube of the base diameters","Cylinder results follow from cone results via XII.10"],"terms":[{"t":"similar cones and cylinders","d":"Those whose axes and base diameters are proportional (XI Def. 24)."}]},{"i":6680,"j":6725,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 13–16","title":"Cylinders cut by planes; polygon between circles","summary":"Proposition 13 shows that a cylinder cut by a plane parallel to its bases is divided in the ratio of the parts of the axis, by the equimultiple test. Proposition 14 proves that cones and cylinders on equal bases are as their heights. Proposition 15 proves that equal cones and cylinders have bases reciprocally proportional to heights, and the converse. Proposition 16 inscribes, in the larger of two concentric circles, an equilateral polygon with an even number of sides that does not touch the smaller circle, preparing the sphere theorems.","ideas":["Cylinder volume is proportional to axis on a fixed base","Equal volume means base and height vary inversely","A polygon can be fitted between two concentric circles"],"terms":[]},{"i":6726,"j":6790,"loc":"Book XII, Propositions 17–18 (with Porism)","title":"Spheres are as cubes of their diameters","summary":"Proposition 17 inscribes, in the larger of two concentric spheres, a polyhedron made of pyramids from the centre that does not touch the smaller sphere, using great circles at right angles and the polygon of XII.16; Euclid proves that the perpendicular from the centre to each face exceeds the smaller radius. The porism shows that similar polyhedra inscribed in two spheres are in the triplicate ratio of the diameters. Proposition 18 then proves by exhaustion that spheres are to one another in the triplicate ratio of their diameters, closing Book XII.","ideas":["A polyhedron can be fitted between two concentric spheres","Similar inscribed polyhedra follow the cube law","Spheres are in the triplicate ratio of diameters"],"terms":[{"t":"greatest circle","d":"A section of a sphere by a plane through its centre, having the sphere's diameter."}]},{"i":6791,"j":6873,"loc":"Book XIII, Historical Note; Propositions 1–5 (with Lemma)","title":"Regular solids' history; extreme and mean ratio","summary":"The editor's note traces the five regular solids to the Pythagoreans and Egyptians, reports a scholium crediting the octahedron and icosahedron to Theaetetus, and mentions Aristaeus' Comparison of the five figures and Apollonius' work on the dodecahedron and icosahedron, as reported by Hypsicles. Euclid's Propositions 1–5 then study a line cut in extreme and mean ratio. The square on the greater segment plus half the whole is five times the square on the half (1), with a converse (2); the lesser segment plus half the greater gives five times the square on that half (3); the squares on the whole and lesser segment are triple the square on the greater (4); and adding the greater segment to the line gives a new line so cut (5).","ideas":["Theaetetus is reported as first to treat all five solids systematically","Book XIII may partly recapitulate Aristaeus","Extreme and mean ratio yields fixed relations of five and three among squares"],"terms":[{"t":"extreme and mean ratio","d":"A cut where the whole is to the greater segment as the greater is to the lesser (VI Def. 3)."},{"t":"gnomon","d":"The L-shaped figure left when a smaller parallelogram is removed from a corner of a larger one."}]},{"i":6874,"j":6931,"loc":"Book XIII, Propositions 6–9","title":"Apotomes, pentagons and the hexagon-decagon line","summary":"Proposition 6 proves that if a rational line is cut in extreme and mean ratio, each segment is an apotome, the greater an apotome and the lesser a first apotome. Proposition 7 shows that an equilateral pentagon with three equal angles, whether adjacent or not, is equiangular. Proposition 8 proves that in a regular pentagon two diagonals subtending adjacent angles cut each other in extreme and mean ratio, with greater segments equal to the side. Proposition 9 proves that the sides of the hexagon and decagon in the same circle, placed together, form a line cut in extreme and mean ratio with the hexagon side as greater segment.","ideas":["The golden cut of a rational line produces irrational segments","Three equal angles suffice to make an equilateral pentagon regular","Pentagon diagonals divide one another in extreme and mean ratio"],"terms":[{"t":"apotome","d":"The irrational remainder when a rational line commensurable only in square with a whole is subtracted from it (X.73)."}]},{"i":6932,"j":6999,"loc":"Book XIII, Propositions 10–12","title":"Pentagon side in terms of hexagon and decagon","summary":"Proposition 10 proves that the square on the side of an equilateral pentagon inscribed in a circle equals the squares on the sides of the hexagon and decagon inscribed in that circle, by two pairs of similar triangles. Proposition 11 proves that if the circle's diameter is rational, the pentagon's side is the irrational line called minor, showing along the way that a related segment is a fourth apotome. Proposition 12 proves that the square on the side of an inscribed equilateral triangle is triple the square on the radius.","ideas":["Pentagon squared equals hexagon squared plus decagon squared","The pentagon's side in a rational circle is a minor line","Triangle side squared is three times the radius squared"],"terms":[{"t":"minor (straight line)","d":"An irrational line of Book X, the side of the square equal to a rational line times a fourth apotome (X.94)."}]},{"i":7000,"j":7066,"loc":"Book XIII, Propositions 13–15 (with Lemma)","title":"Pyramid, octahedron and cube in a sphere","summary":"Euclid constructs a regular pyramid (tetrahedron) and inscribes it in a given sphere, proving that the square on the diameter is one and a half times the square on its side (13); a lemma shows that the parts of a diameter cut by a perpendicular are as the squares on the chords. He then constructs an octahedron in a sphere, with the square on the diameter double the square on its side (14), and a cube, with the square on the diameter triple the square on its side (15). In each case a semicircle rotated on a fixed diameter is shown to pass through every vertex.","ideas":["Each solid is built from a cut of the sphere's diameter","Diameter squared to side squared: 3:2 tetrahedron, 2:1 octahedron, 3:1 cube","Rotating a semicircle on the diameter confirms all vertices lie on the sphere"],"terms":[{"t":"pyramid (here)","d":"The solid of four equilateral triangles, the regular tetrahedron."}]},{"i":7067,"j":7169,"loc":"Book XIII, Propositions 16–17 (with Porisms)","title":"Icosahedron and dodecahedron in a sphere","summary":"Proposition 16 constructs an icosahedron from a regular pentagon, a second rotated pentagon raised by the radius, and two apex points set at decagon-side distances on the axis; XIII.10 shows all twenty triangles are equilateral. It is inscribed in the given sphere, and its side is shown to be the irrational called minor; the porism states the diameter squared is five times the radius of the base circle squared. Proposition 17 builds a dodecahedron by erecting a regular pentagon on each edge of a cube, using extreme and mean ratio cuts, and proves each face equilateral, plane and equiangular. It is inscribed in the sphere about the cube, and its side is proved an apotome; the porism says it is the greater segment of the cube's side so cut.","ideas":["The icosahedron is assembled from pentagons, hexagon sides and decagon sides","The dodecahedron is built as roofs over the twelve edges of a cube","Icosahedron side is minor, dodecahedron side an apotome"],"terms":[{"t":"icosahedron","d":"A solid contained by twenty equal and equilateral triangles (XI Def. 27)."},{"t":"dodecahedron","d":"A solid contained by twelve equal, equilateral and equiangular pentagons (XI Def. 28)."}]},{"i":7170,"j":7225,"loc":"Book XIII, Proposition 18 (with Lemma)","title":"Comparing the five solids; only five exist","summary":"The final proposition sets out on one diameter of a sphere the sides of all five regular solids and compares them. The squares on the sides of pyramid, octahedron and cube stand to the diameter's square as four, three and two parts of six, so these sides are in rational ratios. The icosahedron and dodecahedron sides are irrational (minor and apotome), and the icosahedron's side is proved greater than the dodecahedron's. Euclid then argues that no other solid with equal regular faces exists, since three, four or five triangles, three squares or three pentagons can form a solid angle, but more would reach four right angles. A lemma shows the pentagon's angle is a right angle and a fifth.","ideas":["All five sides are constructed from one sphere diameter","Three sides are rationally related; two are irrational","Only five regular solids are possible because face angles must total under four right angles"],"terms":[]}]});